.TH CREATE_CHTTPSVR_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
create_chttpsvr_mp \- create an HTTP server with a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr create_chttpsvr_mp(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs,
                            clog cl,
                            char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates and initialises a new chttpsvr handle using the given memory
management procedures.  A root router with an empty prefix is created
automatically.

mprocs may be NULL, in which case the default system allocator is used.

cl controls the server's logger and may be NULL:

- cl == NULL: the server creates its own internal logger that writes
  only FATAL messages to stderr.
- cl != NULL: the server derives a new logger from cl (see
  clog_derive) and tags it with the field component=http-server.  The
  derived logger is what the server actually uses and manages; the
  caller's cl handle is left untouched and remains owned by the
  caller.

Either way, the server's own logger (internal or derived) is closed
automatically by __chttpsvr_destroy.

On failure, err_str (if non-NULL) is set to a static diagnostic string
and CHTTPSVR_INVALID is returned.

The supported way to run more than one process serving traffic with
this library is to fork(2) before any server in the process has been
created and started, then have each resulting process, parent and
child alike, call create_chttpsvr_mp/chttpsvr_start independently to
build its own server from scratch; or fork(2) immediately followed by
exec(3) (the pattern behind popen/system/launching a subprocess from
within a request handler), which is fully supported regardless of
whether any server is currently running, since the exec'd program
replaces its process image entirely and never touches anything this
library left behind. Continuing to run without an intervening exec(3)
while a server created before the fork is still live is not a
supported pattern.

As a defense-in-depth measure on top of that, a fresh
create_chttpsvr_mp/chttpsvr_start/_stop/__chttpsvr_destroy/
_register_handler/_register_streaming_handler/_use/_subrouter call,
from any thread, in either the parent or a freshly forked child not
followed by exec(3), never hangs on a lock some other (possibly
no-longer-existing, since fork(2) duplicates only the calling thread)
thread happened to hold at the instant of the fork; this also covers
a fork landing mid-way through starting, stopping, or destroying that
exact handle, or through a chttpsvr_engine_stop()-driven shutdown of
the whole shared engine. Reviving a server already live and actively
running work at the moment of such a fork is a different matter
regardless of that measure, and is explicitly out of scope: its
reactor, worker-pool, and idle-timeout-sweep threads are not
duplicated into the child, so an inherited, already-started server
can no longer accept new connections or process in-flight work there,
and calling chttpsvr_stop() followed by chttpsvr_start() on that
exact handle there does not bring it back either: the call reports
ccol_success, but the server still cannot serve, since the engine
reference it already held going into the fork is reused as-is rather
than rebuilt. This protection can be compiled out with
-DFORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0 for a caller that has no need for it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A valid chttpsvr handle on success, or CHTTPSVR_INVALID on failure.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR create_chttpsvr (3),
.BR __chttpsvr_destroy (3),
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR clog_derive (3)
